{{also|day-light}}
==English==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|day-light}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|daye-lighte}}, {{m|enm|dey liȝht}}, {{m|enm|dailiȝt}}, {{m|enm|day-liht}}, {{m|enm|dai-liht}} (also as {{m|enm|days lyȝt}}, {{m|enm|daies liht}}), equivalent to {{compound|en|day|light}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|Deegeslucht}}, {{m|stq|Daisljoacht|t=daylight}}, {{cog|fy|deiljocht|t=daylight}}, {{cog|nl|daglicht|t=daylight}}, {{cog|de|Tageslicht|t=daylight}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdeɪlaɪt/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈdeɪˌlaɪt/|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-daylight.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-daylight.ogg|a=AU}}
* {{hyphenation|en|day|light}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The light from the [[Sun]], as opposed to that from any other source.
# A light source that [[simulate]]s daylight.
# {{lb|en|countable|photometry}} The intensity distribution of light over the visible spectrum generated by the Sun under various conditions or by other light sources intended to simulate natural daylight.
# The period of time between sunrise and sunset.
#: {{ux|en|We should get home while it's still '''daylight'''.}}
# [[daybreak|Daybreak]].
#: {{ux|en|We had only two hours to work before '''daylight'''.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1835|author=Sir {{w|John Ross (Arctic explorer)|John Ross}}; Sir {{w|James Clark Ross}}|title=[http://books.google.com.au/books?id=SdQBAAAAYAAJ Narrative of a Second Voyage in Search of a North-west Passage &hellip;, Volume 1]|pages=284–5
|passage=Towards the following morning, the thermometer fell to 5&deg;; and at '''daylight''', there was not an atom of water to be seen in any direction.}}
# [[exposure|Exposure]] to public [[scrutiny]].
#: {{ux|en|Budgeting a spy organization can't very well be done in '''daylight'''.}}
# A clear, open space.
#: {{ux|en|All small running backs instinctively run to '''daylight'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|He could barely see '''daylight''' through the complex clockwork.}}
#: {{ux|en|Finally, after weeks of work on the project, they could see '''daylight'''.}}
# {{lb|en|countable|machinery}} The space between [[platen]]s on a [[press]] or similar machinery.
#: {{ux|en|The minimum and maximum '''daylights''' on an injection molding machine determines the sizes of the items it can make.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} Emotional or psychological distance between people, or [[disagreement]].
#: {{ux|en|We completely agree. There's no '''daylight''' between us on the issue.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Michelle Goldberg|title=What Fox News Says When You’re Not Listening|work=The New York Times|date=2023-02-17|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2023/02/17/opinion/fox-news-dominion.html|issn=0362-4331|passage=Sometimes hosts are a little saltier when the cameras aren’t rolling, but I don’t recall ever hearing any '''daylight''' between the views they express on-air and off.}}
# {{lb|en|figurative}} Meaningful or noticeable difference or distinction between two things, especially concepts.
#: {{ux|en|There's not much '''daylight''' between saying that the universe is God and saying that God doesn't exist.}}
# The gap between the top of a drinking-glass and the level of drink it is filled with.
#: {{cot|en|heeltap}}

